WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.900
An enforcement point is where the application applies an authorization decision.

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:19.000
Python applications may enforce access at route level, view level, service layer, data layer, middleware, decorators, dependency injection hooks, guard clauses, reusable helpers, or centralized policy checks.

3
00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:22.380
Each location has strengths and limits.

4
00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:31.200
A route-level check may ensure the caller is authenticated and has a broad role, while a service-layer check may understand the specific resource and action.

5
00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:33.560
Server-side enforcement is essential.

6
00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:43.420
Client-side checks, hidden buttons, disabled controls, and hidden routes can improve user experience, but they do not protect sensitive operations by themselves.

7
00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:51.840
Requests can come from scripts, direct API calls, background jobs, alternate clients, or modified browsers.

8
00:00:51.940 --> 00:01:00.660
Trusted application logic must enforce decisions before returning sensitive data, changing state, launching jobs, or invoking privileged integrations.

9
00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:04.300
Reusable patterns help prevent scattered one-off checks.

10
00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:08.960
Decorators or dependencies can be useful for common route guards.

11
00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.260
Middleware can ensure authentication state is present.

12
00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.280
Service-layer helpers can centralize object-specific checks.

13
00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:19.260
Data-layer filters can limit list results.

14
00:01:19.360 --> 00:01:25.280
Centralized policy functions can give reviewers one place to inspect high-value rules.

15
00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:31.940
The goal is not to put every rule in one file; it is to make the pattern predictable and hard to forget.

16
00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:36.940
Enforcement should happen before sensitive actions and before data disclosure.

17
00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:43.040
A list endpoint should not fetch every record and rely on the frontend to hide unauthorized items.

18
00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:48.260
An export job should not start before checking the caller's permission for the selected tenant and resource.

19
00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:56.040
A background worker should not assume that the scheduling user had permission unless that authorization decision is preserved and rechecked as needed.

20
00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:59.980
Good enforcement is close to risk, not merely close to routing.
